WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.654 --> 00:00:01.963
- Why is it certain businesses

00:00:01.963 --> 00:00:04.337
can hover above their competition?

00:00:04.337 --> 00:00:06.893
I mean, is Starbucks really
serving the best cup of coffee?

00:00:06.893 --> 00:00:09.976
I mean, to want, and to be ambitious,

00:00:10.893 --> 00:00:14.807
and to want to be
successful is not enough.

00:00:14.807 --> 00:00:18.101
Very often the thing
that actors tend to do,

00:00:18.101 --> 00:00:20.581
is I think they tend to
focus on the wrong thing.

00:00:20.581 --> 00:00:23.231
And every time I take on something,

00:00:23.231 --> 00:00:26.466
the only thing that interests
me is what scares me.

00:00:26.466 --> 00:00:29.490
- He's an American actor,
film director, producer,

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:31.429
comedian, and singer.

00:00:31.429 --> 00:00:34.660
He began his career as a
stage actor in the 1980s.

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:37.095
Since 2013, he's played Frank Underwood

00:00:37.095 --> 00:00:39.117
on the Netflix series House of Cards,

00:00:39.117 --> 00:00:40.860
for which he's won numerous awards.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:41.850
He's Kevin Spacey,

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:44.567
and here's my take on his
top 10 rules for success.

00:00:44.567 --> 00:00:46.134
Rule number eight is my personal favorite.

00:00:46.134 --> 00:00:47.967
And make sure to stick around
all the way til the end

00:00:47.967 --> 00:00:49.319
for some special bonus clips.

00:00:49.319 --> 00:00:50.428
Also, as Kevin is talking,

00:00:50.428 --> 00:00:52.659
if he says something that
really is meaningful to you,

00:00:52.659 --> 00:00:54.547
make sure to write it down
in the comments below,

00:00:54.547 --> 00:00:57.881
and put quotes around it so
others can be inspired as well.

00:00:57.881 --> 00:01:01.014
(jet engine)

00:01:01.014 --> 00:01:03.545
- Well we all are in the
business of storytelling.

00:01:03.545 --> 00:01:05.322
I mean it's not just
exclusive for people like me

00:01:05.322 --> 00:01:06.761
who are in the entertainment business.

00:01:06.761 --> 00:01:08.895
I think that, no matter
what business you're in,

00:01:08.895 --> 00:01:12.342
no matter what you're selling or doing.

00:01:12.342 --> 00:01:14.280
Why is it certain businesses can hover

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.259
above their competition?

00:01:16.259 --> 00:01:20.379
I mean is Starbucks really
serving the best cup of coffee?

00:01:20.379 --> 00:01:21.322
Maybe not.

00:01:21.322 --> 00:01:25.020
I mean, I like a Caramel Macchiato myself.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:25.853
But is American Express

00:01:25.853 --> 00:01:27.834
really the best return
rate on a credit card?

00:01:27.834 --> 00:01:29.851
Hmm, probably not.

00:01:29.851 --> 00:01:32.612
But it's the story they manage to tell.

00:01:32.612 --> 00:01:35.737
And I think that the more
that we think about that,

00:01:35.737 --> 00:01:37.154
the more that we.

00:01:38.422 --> 00:01:40.068
Sometimes we see commercials,

00:01:40.068 --> 00:01:43.473
and we see stuff and we
just go, it's a turn off.

00:01:43.473 --> 00:01:46.186
But somehow, those people
that manage to tell a story,

00:01:46.186 --> 00:01:50.852
manage to even temporarily
move us in a certain way,

00:01:50.852 --> 00:01:52.843
that we remember.

00:01:52.843 --> 00:01:54.272
We don't feel like we're being sold,

00:01:54.272 --> 00:01:56.025
we feel like we're being told something

00:01:56.025 --> 00:01:57.103
that we can connect to.

00:01:57.103 --> 00:02:00.198
So I've always been
driven by that very idea,

00:02:00.198 --> 00:02:01.430
that very notion.

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:05.026
- Can we help us appreciate
this early experience,

00:02:05.026 --> 00:02:07.815
these lean years, these
very difficult years

00:02:07.815 --> 00:02:10.565
leading up to the ultimate prize.

00:02:11.674 --> 00:02:14.257
- There is no prize, out there.

00:02:15.738 --> 00:02:18.500
The only prize is this one,

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:23.052
and what you feel, and what
you want to accomplish.

00:02:23.052 --> 00:02:26.524
And if you can, as you start out,

00:02:26.524 --> 00:02:30.691
these what could be lean
years or could be fat years.

00:02:34.115 --> 00:02:38.970
I feel that I very often
watch a lot of young people

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:42.069
sort of meander around without any idea

00:02:42.069 --> 00:02:44.886
about why they're doing
what they're doing.

00:02:44.886 --> 00:02:47.886
I mean, to want and to be ambitious,

00:02:48.903 --> 00:02:52.570
and to want to be
successful, is not enough.

00:02:53.822 --> 00:02:55.405
That's just desire.

00:02:56.318 --> 00:02:58.151
To know what you want,

00:02:59.021 --> 00:03:01.645
to understand why you're doing it,

00:03:01.645 --> 00:03:05.728
to dedicate every breath
in your body to achieve.

00:03:09.959 --> 00:03:12.327
If you feel you have something to give,

00:03:12.327 --> 00:03:15.672
if you feel that your particular talent

00:03:15.672 --> 00:03:19.089
is worth developing, is worth caring for,

00:03:20.536 --> 00:03:22.804
then there's nothing you can' achieve.

00:03:22.804 --> 00:03:25.123
And I think that when I
was beginning as an actor,

00:03:25.123 --> 00:03:27.472
I was fortunate to have gone to Julliard,

00:03:27.472 --> 00:03:29.197
when I did go study drama,

00:03:29.197 --> 00:03:31.323
with a great number of
actors and actresses

00:03:31.323 --> 00:03:35.106
who were going on to become
very well known, very quickly.

00:03:35.106 --> 00:03:36.189
And I wasn't.

00:03:37.491 --> 00:03:39.903
I think the sort of greatest lesson

00:03:39.903 --> 00:03:41.876
I came out of about that,

00:03:41.876 --> 00:03:44.007
was that comparing
yourself to other people

00:03:44.007 --> 00:03:45.398
is a real mistake.

00:03:45.398 --> 00:03:49.185
I mean my struggle
wasn't any worse or less,

00:03:49.185 --> 00:03:50.520
or whatever than anyone else's.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:51.903
It was just mine.

00:03:51.903 --> 00:03:55.310
I think the audition
process is no different

00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:59.751
than going for a meeting
as a cinematographer,

00:03:59.751 --> 00:04:03.918
if you want to be in any of the
disciplines of this industry.

00:04:04.792 --> 00:04:08.126
Very often, the thing
that actors tend to do,

00:04:08.126 --> 00:04:11.718
is I think they tend to
focus on the wrong thing.

00:04:11.718 --> 00:04:12.704
So if I were to say to you,

00:04:12.704 --> 00:04:15.261
what do you think the
purpose of an audition is,

00:04:15.261 --> 00:04:17.626
what would your answer be?

00:04:17.626 --> 00:04:20.828
What's the purpose of an audition?

00:04:20.828 --> 00:04:22.191
- [Audience Member] To live
truthfully in the moment&gt;

00:04:22.191 --> 00:04:24.338
- To live truthfully in
the moment, very nice.

00:04:24.338 --> 00:04:26.801
Most people would say to
get the (expletive) job.

00:04:26.801 --> 00:04:28.599
(laughing)

00:04:28.599 --> 00:04:29.513
Alright.

00:04:29.513 --> 00:04:33.680
That is a very, very, very
dangerous place to start,

00:04:37.072 --> 00:04:38.585
to want to get the job.

00:04:38.585 --> 00:04:41.271
Now, look, let's face facts.

00:04:41.271 --> 00:04:42.526
If you get the job, how great.

00:04:42.526 --> 00:04:44.239
That's (expletive) great,
it's icing on the cake;

00:04:44.239 --> 00:04:45.489
it's fantastic.

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:48.673
But I believe that if actors

00:04:51.443 --> 00:04:54.860
were better willing to adopt a philosophy

00:04:57.869 --> 00:05:01.053
that it is an opportunity
to walk into a room,

00:05:01.053 --> 00:05:03.391
and to begin a relationship

00:05:03.391 --> 00:05:07.224
that may not pay off
with that particular job.

00:05:08.605 --> 00:05:11.520
Sometimes actors are so nervous,

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:13.281
and they want that job so much

00:05:13.281 --> 00:05:14.446
or they can't pay their (expletive) rent.

00:05:14.446 --> 00:05:15.279
They got to get that (expletive) job.

00:05:15.279 --> 00:05:16.112
I got to get that (expletive) job.

00:05:16.112 --> 00:05:17.029
Hi, hi, hi.

00:05:19.003 --> 00:05:20.420
Well we see that.

00:05:21.383 --> 00:05:24.169
You know, the director sees that.

00:05:24.169 --> 00:05:28.336
So the problem with that is
that you're not being yourself.

00:05:29.621 --> 00:05:31.772
You can't be truthful in the moment

00:05:31.772 --> 00:05:34.605
when you're focus is on the result

00:05:35.924 --> 00:05:37.979
instead of what, on
that day at that moment,

00:05:37.979 --> 00:05:40.538
is the beginning of a process.

00:05:40.538 --> 00:05:43.982
Can I work with this actor,
the director's thinking.

00:05:43.982 --> 00:05:46.727
Does this actor pay attention?

00:05:46.727 --> 00:05:48.188
Do they listen?

00:05:48.188 --> 00:05:49.918
And when I give them direction,

00:05:49.918 --> 00:05:52.647
do they actually change anything?

00:05:52.647 --> 00:05:54.822
Do they take direction?

00:05:54.822 --> 00:05:55.822
Go that way.

00:05:57.948 --> 00:06:01.198
And if you can stand in a room and say,

00:06:03.719 --> 00:06:05.928
I may be raw, I may be undeveloped,

00:06:05.928 --> 00:06:08.444
I may have no experience,

00:06:08.444 --> 00:06:11.139
but I trust myself enough,

00:06:11.139 --> 00:06:14.605
and I believe in myself
enough on this day,

00:06:14.605 --> 00:06:17.452
that I want to introduce
myself to this director,

00:06:17.452 --> 00:06:20.776
casting director, producer, assistant,

00:06:20.776 --> 00:06:25.100
and by the way, be really
nice to the assistants.

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:29.267
Because no one behind that
table is any different than you.

00:06:30.679 --> 00:06:34.757
Everyone behind that table is
just as ambitious as you are.

00:06:34.757 --> 00:06:37.002
That director wants to
be doing better things.

00:06:37.002 --> 00:06:39.628
That casting director
wants to be a director.

00:06:39.628 --> 00:06:43.738
That assistant wants to
be the casting director.

00:06:43.738 --> 00:06:45.753
Everybody wants to be
somewhere else in 10 years,

00:06:45.753 --> 00:06:47.125
just like you do.

00:06:47.125 --> 00:06:49.476
So we're all in the same boat.

00:06:49.476 --> 00:06:52.809
And it's sometimes hard to believe this.

00:06:54.021 --> 00:06:56.838
No matter how rude they might be,

00:06:56.838 --> 00:06:58.355
no matter how;

00:06:58.355 --> 00:07:03.158
'cause some of them are
just; they're (expletive)

00:07:03.158 --> 00:07:04.858
in those kinds of situations.

00:07:04.858 --> 00:07:05.911
My point is this.

00:07:05.911 --> 00:07:07.661
They're on your side.

00:07:08.879 --> 00:07:12.773
Because they have to cast this thing.

00:07:12.773 --> 00:07:15.521
And because people are fundamentally lazy,

00:07:15.521 --> 00:07:19.572
they'd rather be home
smoking pot and watching TV.

00:07:19.572 --> 00:07:22.572
(audience clapping)

00:07:28.991 --> 00:07:31.074
They want you to be good.

00:07:32.411 --> 00:07:35.494
They want you to solve their problem.

00:07:36.844 --> 00:07:40.835
And too often, I think
people don't realize that

00:07:40.835 --> 00:07:43.712
you have to take the long view.

00:07:43.712 --> 00:07:45.960
The short view is I want
this job, I need this job,

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:46.993
I want this job.

00:07:46.993 --> 00:07:51.222
The long view is, I have
a chance to meet someone

00:07:51.222 --> 00:07:53.101
I may admire, someone I could work with.

00:07:53.101 --> 00:07:55.809
But I may not be right
for this job on this day.

00:07:55.809 --> 00:07:58.797
That has nothing to do with my talent.

00:07:58.797 --> 00:08:03.239
It could be a thousand reasons
why you don't get a job.

00:08:03.239 --> 00:08:04.933
But if I'm good enough,

00:08:04.933 --> 00:08:06.627
if I stand on that stage

00:08:06.627 --> 00:08:09.977
and I show them where my
talent is on this day,

00:08:09.977 --> 00:08:12.326
I walk out that door, they
might look at each other and go,

00:08:12.326 --> 00:08:14.633
"She's really good.

00:08:14.633 --> 00:08:15.595
"She's really good.

00:08:15.595 --> 00:08:17.212
"She's not right for this,
but she's really good."

00:08:17.212 --> 00:08:18.860
And the next time they do a film,

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:20.363
or the next time they do a play,

00:08:20.363 --> 00:08:22.280
they'll bring you back.

00:08:23.162 --> 00:08:25.763
And someday along that long road,

00:08:25.763 --> 00:08:28.332
you may get that job.

00:08:28.332 --> 00:08:30.482
But it's about relationships,

00:08:30.482 --> 00:08:33.866
and it's about trusting
yourself in where you are.

00:08:33.866 --> 00:08:36.074
And realizing you are good
enough for where you are

00:08:36.074 --> 00:08:38.078
right now on that day.

00:08:38.078 --> 00:08:39.970
And I believe in the long view.

00:08:39.970 --> 00:08:41.487
And I also believe that audiences

00:08:41.487 --> 00:08:44.231
have remarkable attention spans.

00:08:44.231 --> 00:08:45.375
You know, there's always
this sort of argument.

00:08:45.375 --> 00:08:47.878
You sometimes see it in
the way movies are cut.

00:08:47.878 --> 00:08:49.726
Where there's a cut every three seconds,

00:08:49.726 --> 00:08:53.376
because God forbid something
isn't happening on screen;

00:08:53.376 --> 00:08:55.220
something isn't distracting.

00:08:55.220 --> 00:08:56.757
But if you go back and
look at the great movies,

00:08:56.757 --> 00:08:58.475
there's just a camera,

00:08:58.475 --> 00:09:00.808
capturing people in a space.

00:09:03.477 --> 00:09:05.060
That is compelling.

00:09:06.130 --> 00:09:08.860
And I believe audiences,
actually at the end of the day,

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:11.005
they don't care about platforms.

00:09:11.005 --> 00:09:14.058
They don't care that it's
on Netflix or on Instagram,

00:09:14.058 --> 00:09:18.516
or a six second Vine, or a
13 hour mini-series on PBS.

00:09:18.516 --> 00:09:21.263
They want good stories.

00:09:21.263 --> 00:09:25.430
If we give them great content,
not just to fill the time,

00:09:26.817 --> 00:09:28.677
but great content that are great stories,

00:09:28.677 --> 00:09:31.734
they will watch it on
whatever platform it's on.

00:09:31.734 --> 00:09:33.672
- But there is something
different in a story,

00:09:33.672 --> 00:09:37.011
a complexity that's possible,
obviously with time.

00:09:37.011 --> 00:09:38.171
And do you think that there is,

00:09:38.171 --> 00:09:41.708
if you like, a swing back against

00:09:41.708 --> 00:09:45.860
short, snappier, bite-size
pieces of content,

00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:47.855
to something that is much more textured?

00:09:47.855 --> 00:09:50.286
- No, I actually think
there's room for all of it.

00:09:50.286 --> 00:09:51.569
And I think that that's why we're seeing

00:09:51.569 --> 00:09:54.444
so many different kinds
of platforms emerge.

00:09:54.444 --> 00:09:56.462
You know, people are adapting storytelling

00:09:56.462 --> 00:09:58.819
for the platform that they're on.

00:09:58.819 --> 00:10:00.091
And that's actually kind of exciting,

00:10:00.091 --> 00:10:01.699
and opening up new doors.

00:10:01.699 --> 00:10:04.201
I travel, I play a lot of tennis,
I play a lot of ping-pong,

00:10:04.201 --> 00:10:07.626
I see a lot of friends
and make a lot of dinners.

00:10:07.626 --> 00:10:08.756
I enjoy my life.

00:10:08.756 --> 00:10:11.886
In fact, in the last seven
years I've carved out more time

00:10:11.886 --> 00:10:15.543
for me than I think
ever before in my life.

00:10:15.543 --> 00:10:16.572
I think you have to.

00:10:16.572 --> 00:10:19.274
I think it's hugely important
to be able to balance

00:10:19.274 --> 00:10:21.040
your life with your work.

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:23.068
I'm loving living life,

00:10:23.068 --> 00:10:25.231
and I think it's just hugely important.

00:10:25.231 --> 00:10:28.198
I'm fortunate, I don't
have a nine to five job,

00:10:28.198 --> 00:10:30.555
but this job does require

00:10:30.555 --> 00:10:31.922
an enormous amount of my attention,

00:10:31.922 --> 00:10:33.939
and I live full-time in London.

00:10:33.939 --> 00:10:36.492
And I only leave London if
I'm going to make a movie.

00:10:36.492 --> 00:10:39.322
But even if I'm able
to carve out two hours

00:10:39.322 --> 00:10:41.213
in the middle of the
afternoon to go play tennis,

00:10:41.213 --> 00:10:44.018
or go to a gallery or take a walk,

00:10:44.018 --> 00:10:45.978
that's hugely valuable.

00:10:45.978 --> 00:10:49.194
Even a two-day weekend can
feel like a five-day holiday.

00:10:49.194 --> 00:10:52.539
- [Interviewer] You've worked
with unbelievable directors

00:10:52.539 --> 00:10:55.215
and other actors, and on great work,

00:10:55.215 --> 00:10:56.882
Mamas work, O'Neill.

00:10:57.996 --> 00:11:00.800
Can you just talk about that,
at all about the collaboration

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:02.101
or about the --

00:11:02.101 --> 00:11:03.601
- Well that is it.

00:11:04.707 --> 00:11:08.874
That is the key to doing
anything, and doing it well.

00:11:11.050 --> 00:11:13.581
Either in film or in theater.

00:11:13.581 --> 00:11:17.650
You know it's about bringing
all the elements together.

00:11:17.650 --> 00:11:19.663
And if you are fortunate enough

00:11:19.663 --> 00:11:22.413
to not be driven by your own ego,

00:11:23.862 --> 00:11:27.614
and not believe that all your
ideas are the only good ones,

00:11:27.614 --> 00:11:31.281
and are open to suggestion
and to criticism,

00:11:33.036 --> 00:11:35.493
that's not a good idea.

00:11:35.493 --> 00:11:37.516
Don't play it that way.

00:11:37.516 --> 00:11:39.542
That doesn't serve the writing.

00:11:39.542 --> 00:11:43.709
As long as actors, and to
a large degree directors,

00:11:44.739 --> 00:11:47.322
our job is to serve the writer.

00:11:49.372 --> 00:11:51.430
Serve the writing.

00:11:51.430 --> 00:11:52.486
If you serve the writing,

00:11:52.486 --> 00:11:54.079
you will ultimately serve yourself.

00:11:54.079 --> 00:11:58.210
I think we have gone
through a couple of decades

00:11:58.210 --> 00:12:00.439
where actors have been spending more time

00:12:00.439 --> 00:12:02.797
serving themselves.

00:12:02.797 --> 00:12:05.576
And they have forgotten that
it is about the writing.

00:12:05.576 --> 00:12:07.800
It is about finding out the world

00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:09.966
that a writer wants to create,

00:12:09.966 --> 00:12:12.027
and doing everything you
can to give the director

00:12:12.027 --> 00:12:15.906
as many choices as they
possibly can have in editing.

00:12:15.906 --> 00:12:18.003
And collaborating and being open about it,

00:12:18.003 --> 00:12:20.024
and loving that aspect of it.

00:12:20.024 --> 00:12:22.274
Because it's so incredible.

00:12:24.119 --> 00:12:27.179
If you A, trust the
people that you work with,

00:12:27.179 --> 00:12:30.929
and bring an atmosphere
of openness and humor

00:12:32.607 --> 00:12:35.957
to the proceedings so that
everybody wants to come to work

00:12:35.957 --> 00:12:38.854
everyday and do their best job.

00:12:38.854 --> 00:12:40.520
So it is about collaboration.

00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:44.086
There's lots of films that people know

00:12:44.086 --> 00:12:46.952
that are always sort of mentioned as,

00:12:46.952 --> 00:12:48.356
wasn't that your favorite?

00:12:48.356 --> 00:12:50.255
I hope I haven't done it yet.

00:12:50.255 --> 00:12:52.993
And every time I take on something,

00:12:52.993 --> 00:12:56.188
the only thing that interests
me is what scares me.

00:12:56.188 --> 00:12:59.068
The only thing that interests
me is what I think I can't do.

00:12:59.068 --> 00:13:00.653
Well when you're doing a TV show,

00:13:00.653 --> 00:13:02.862
you know everyday is a different day

00:13:02.862 --> 00:13:04.707
in the sense that it's never the same.

00:13:04.707 --> 00:13:06.547
And you might be shooting two scenes,

00:13:06.547 --> 00:13:09.231
you might have one scene,
you might have five scenes.

00:13:09.231 --> 00:13:13.398
So your level of preparation
is different every time.

00:13:15.034 --> 00:13:17.895
Generally, I'm up early,
I exercise, I run,

00:13:17.895 --> 00:13:21.139
I do whatever it is I'm doing
in terms of my physical life

00:13:21.139 --> 00:13:22.108
other than playing tennis,

00:13:22.108 --> 00:13:25.590
which I stack up during
the course of a week,

00:13:25.590 --> 00:13:28.043
quite a lot of if I can.

00:13:28.043 --> 00:13:30.833
And you know, get to set,
depending on your call time,

00:13:30.833 --> 00:13:33.661
but very often it's very early.

00:13:33.661 --> 00:13:36.454
And then you try to be there

00:13:36.454 --> 00:13:39.742
and be alert and awake,
and alive and ready to go

00:13:39.742 --> 00:13:41.716
every hour of the day.

00:13:41.716 --> 00:13:45.489
And if you work 12 hours,
then you have to be alert

00:13:45.489 --> 00:13:48.032
every one of those hours.

00:13:48.032 --> 00:13:51.390
It's an important thing to stay focused.

00:13:51.390 --> 00:13:53.723
- When someone has the gift,

00:13:56.579 --> 00:13:57.912
I cannot resist.

00:13:58.945 --> 00:14:02.112
I am told that you are so adept at it.

00:14:03.652 --> 00:14:05.259
If our audience closes its eyes,

00:14:05.259 --> 00:14:06.674
they will actually have the impression

00:14:06.674 --> 00:14:08.941
that I am privileged to
interview some people

00:14:08.941 --> 00:14:13.099
who might otherwise never
have appeared on this stage.

00:14:13.099 --> 00:14:15.349
Our guest is Jimmy Stewart.

00:14:16.927 --> 00:14:18.761
And Mr. Stewart, generations of actors

00:14:18.761 --> 00:14:21.645
have admired your naturalism,
and your simplicity.

00:14:21.645 --> 00:14:24.381
Now is that just Jimmy Stewart,

00:14:24.381 --> 00:14:28.287
or is it Jimmy Stewart playing
a kind of Jimmy Stewart?

00:14:28.287 --> 00:14:30.537
(laughing)

00:14:35.408 --> 00:14:39.703
- Well now, Jimmy, I
know what you're saying.

00:14:39.703 --> 00:14:43.067
I'm aware of what you're trying to say.

00:14:43.067 --> 00:14:45.435
And the answer is yes.

00:14:45.435 --> 00:14:47.685
(clapping)

00:14:55.784 --> 00:14:57.471
- You can all testify now to the fact

00:14:57.471 --> 00:15:00.638
that I have interviewed Jimmy Stewart.

00:15:02.362 --> 00:15:05.335
Here I am; this is after all
a kind of interview show,

00:15:05.335 --> 00:15:06.566
a talk show.

00:15:06.566 --> 00:15:09.354
And Johnny, you reigned for years

00:15:09.354 --> 00:15:12.312
as king of talk show hosts.

00:15:12.312 --> 00:15:14.229
- Oh now we're talking.

00:15:15.169 --> 00:15:18.786
- What was the secret of your success?

00:15:18.786 --> 00:15:21.036
(laughing)

00:15:24.793 --> 00:15:26.316
- You know Jimmy, I'd
love to answer you that,

00:15:26.316 --> 00:15:28.321
but I don't work on Mondays.

00:15:28.321 --> 00:15:30.571
(clapping)

00:15:39.031 --> 00:15:41.023
- I understand that you have conducted

00:15:41.023 --> 00:15:44.356
a long correspondence with Kevin Spacey,

00:15:47.485 --> 00:15:51.235
and that he approached
you once, Ms. Hepburn.

00:15:52.471 --> 00:15:55.851
And I would like to
know, what's that story.

00:15:55.851 --> 00:15:56.811
Do you actually correspond?

00:15:56.811 --> 00:15:59.948
What do you --
- He's a stalker.

00:15:59.948 --> 00:16:02.198
(clapping)

00:16:07.541 --> 00:16:09.958
He just won't leave me alone.

00:16:11.182 --> 00:16:13.993
- When he writes to you,
what do you respond?

00:16:13.993 --> 00:16:16.610
- He writes letters, and
goes on and on about himself.

00:16:16.610 --> 00:16:19.513
And I just say, "Good for you."

00:16:19.513 --> 00:16:21.763
(clapping)

00:16:30.910 --> 00:16:32.225
- I would like to ask you please,

00:16:32.225 --> 00:16:33.617
because you are a very good director,

00:16:33.617 --> 00:16:37.670
as well as a wonderful actor Mr. Eastwood.

00:16:37.670 --> 00:16:41.342
What is the word of advice
for our many directors

00:16:41.342 --> 00:16:43.342
out there in our school.

00:16:45.202 --> 00:16:47.452
(laughing)

00:16:48.465 --> 00:16:50.410
- Well I'd say get it done by four,

00:16:50.410 --> 00:16:52.996
so you can hit the links.

00:16:52.996 --> 00:16:56.874
- (laughing) Mr. Gielgud,
am I on the right track.

00:16:56.874 --> 00:16:59.362
Mr. Gielgud, American actors envy you.

00:16:59.362 --> 00:17:02.131
Your impeccable diction,
how did you acquire it.

00:17:02.131 --> 00:17:06.240
- Oh went to school, you know,
trained for years and years.

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:08.490
(clapping)

00:17:11.119 --> 00:17:12.282
- Marlon.

00:17:12.282 --> 00:17:14.942
(laughing)

00:17:14.942 --> 00:17:18.442
English actors do not admire your diction.

00:17:19.770 --> 00:17:20.862
How did you acquire it?

00:17:20.862 --> 00:17:25.113
Could it by any chance have
been a result of your training

00:17:25.113 --> 00:17:26.946
at the Actor's Studio?

00:17:29.104 --> 00:17:32.500
- Listen, I didn't learn twiddly
twat at the Actor's Studio.

00:17:32.500 --> 00:17:34.750
(cheering)

00:17:42.724 --> 00:17:45.831
(laughing) And frankly,
I'm just sick and tired

00:17:45.831 --> 00:17:49.831
of all you cats just
living off my name forever.

00:17:51.133 --> 00:17:55.366
So leave me alone, and bring
me the Doritos would you?

00:17:55.366 --> 00:17:57.616
(clapping)

00:18:02.429 --> 00:18:04.956
- Mr. Walken, you are.

00:18:04.956 --> 00:18:07.206
(cheering)

00:18:08.521 --> 00:18:10.270
You are, despite what was just said,

00:18:10.270 --> 00:18:12.510
a distinguished member
of the Actor's Studio,

00:18:12.510 --> 00:18:14.919
and this is your second
time on this stage.

00:18:14.919 --> 00:18:17.248
Do you have a response for Marlon.

00:18:17.248 --> 00:18:18.581
- Well you know.

00:18:24.376 --> 00:18:26.007
He's just stoned you know.

00:18:26.007 --> 00:18:28.674
You got to forgive him, you know.

00:18:30.073 --> 00:18:31.740
He's crazy that way.

00:18:35.103 --> 00:18:36.614
- I'm going to call on
another member of the

00:18:36.614 --> 00:18:37.625
Actor's Studio, distinguished member

00:18:37.625 --> 00:18:40.458
of the Actor's Studio, to respond.

00:18:41.484 --> 00:18:42.401
Mr. Pacino.

00:18:45.907 --> 00:18:49.907
You've already been
immortalized by Kevin Spacey

00:18:53.618 --> 00:18:54.763
on Saturday Night Live.

00:18:54.763 --> 00:18:56.210
We all saw that.

00:18:56.210 --> 00:18:57.634
How did you feel about it?

00:18:57.634 --> 00:18:58.587
Were you pleased?

00:18:58.587 --> 00:18:59.504
- Oh funny.

00:19:00.704 --> 00:19:02.954
(cheering)

00:19:16.823 --> 00:19:18.309
- Didn't bother you at all?

00:19:18.309 --> 00:19:19.142
- No.

00:19:21.215 --> 00:19:23.132
Can I get a cappuccino?

00:19:27.548 --> 00:19:31.048
- I know that like so many of our students

00:19:32.415 --> 00:19:36.582
who remember your evening
here on this stage with us.

00:19:39.127 --> 00:19:42.307
Kevin Spacey often
looks to you Jack Lemmon

00:19:42.307 --> 00:19:44.500
for professional wisdom.

00:19:44.500 --> 00:19:46.899
What is your final word tonight?

00:19:46.899 --> 00:19:48.464
What is your advice to our youngsters?

00:19:48.464 --> 00:19:49.753
- I wish to hell he'd stop calling me,

00:19:49.753 --> 00:19:52.051
and leave me the (expletive) alone.

00:19:52.051 --> 00:19:54.681
(jet engine)

00:19:54.681 --> 00:19:55.721
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:19:55.721 --> 00:19:58.896
I made this video because
Dante Deluca asked me to.

00:19:58.896 --> 00:20:00.058
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:20:00.058 --> 00:20:01.595
that you want me to profile next,

00:20:01.595 --> 00:20:02.745
leave it in the comments below,

00:20:02.745 --> 00:20:03.876
and I'll see what I can do.

00:20:03.876 --> 00:20:05.366
I'd also love to know
which of the 10 rules

00:20:05.366 --> 00:20:06.712
had the biggest impact on you;

00:20:06.712 --> 00:20:07.662
what change are you going to make

00:20:07.662 --> 00:20:09.116
as a result of watching this video?

00:20:09.116 --> 00:20:11.965
Leave it in the comments and
I will join in the discussion.

00:20:11.965 --> 00:20:13.098
Finally, I wanted to
give a quick shout out

00:20:13.098 --> 00:20:15.779
to Anna Weber from
SuccessfullyPublished.com.

00:20:15.779 --> 00:20:17.696
Anna, thank you so much
for buying my book.

00:20:17.696 --> 00:20:19.038
It really means a lot to me.

00:20:19.038 --> 00:20:21.159
For those of you who want
a chance at a (mumbles)

00:20:21.159 --> 00:20:22.534
and some bonuses in a a future video,

00:20:22.534 --> 00:20:24.471
make sure to pick up a copy of the book,

00:20:24.471 --> 00:20:26.578
and email in your receipt
so we can keep track.

00:20:26.578 --> 00:20:28.084
Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:20:28.084 --> 00:20:30.855
Continue to believe, or
whatever your one word is,

00:20:30.855 --> 00:20:32.725
and I'll see you soon.

00:20:32.725 --> 00:20:35.142
(jet engine)

00:20:38.582 --> 00:20:41.257
- I can tell you the moment
I did the very first thing

00:20:41.257 --> 00:20:45.870
in drama class where we had
to do a pantomime to music.

00:20:45.870 --> 00:20:50.518
And I created a western scene
where I was riding a horse,

00:20:50.518 --> 00:20:52.067
I had a six gun,

00:20:52.067 --> 00:20:55.734
and it was to the music
from Dueling Banjos.

00:20:57.933 --> 00:20:59.565
So the horse got faster
and faster as I went.

00:20:59.565 --> 00:21:02.441
And everybody applauded and laughed.

00:21:02.441 --> 00:21:06.024
And I was like, well
that's a sound I like.

00:21:07.985 --> 00:21:09.535
And then the drama teacher there,

00:21:09.535 --> 00:21:14.187
who was really remarkable,
and a great mentor,

00:21:14.187 --> 00:21:17.085
then led me toward a workshop,

00:21:17.085 --> 00:21:19.666
that was being run by one of my idols,

00:21:19.666 --> 00:21:21.880
which was Jack Lemmon.

00:21:21.880 --> 00:21:26.047
I mean his work in Some Like
It Hot, or the Apartment,

00:21:27.081 --> 00:21:29.910
The Odd Couple; some of the
finest work I'd ever seen.

00:21:29.910 --> 00:21:31.926
He just did comedy and drama brilliantly.

00:21:31.926 --> 00:21:34.176
He was a kind of every man.

00:21:35.234 --> 00:21:37.675
So he was running this workshop
at the (mumbles) Perform

00:21:37.675 --> 00:21:39.861
in 1974, and I got a chance to go.

00:21:39.861 --> 00:21:40.896
And one of the things we had to do,

00:21:40.896 --> 00:21:42.612
was we had to do scenes.

00:21:42.612 --> 00:21:45.536
And Mr. Lemmon was going
to critique our scenes.

00:21:45.536 --> 00:21:49.703
And so I did a scene; I was
very shy, I was very nervous.

00:21:52.302 --> 00:21:54.348
And he came up to me after I did my scene,

00:21:54.348 --> 00:21:55.594
he put his hand on my
shoulder and he said,

00:21:55.594 --> 00:21:57.228
"That was a touch of terrific."

00:21:57.228 --> 00:21:59.993
(laughing)

00:21:59.993 --> 00:22:02.509
He said, "No, I'm not
kidding, what's your name?"

00:22:02.509 --> 00:22:03.342
I said, "Kevin."

00:22:03.342 --> 00:22:04.693
He said, "This young actor has been doing

00:22:04.693 --> 00:22:06.149
"what I've been talking
about all afternoon.

00:22:06.149 --> 00:22:08.600
"He was connected to the material.

00:22:08.600 --> 00:22:11.120
"I'm telling you that you
should go to New York,

00:22:11.120 --> 00:22:12.324
"and you should study to be an actor,

00:22:12.324 --> 00:22:16.788
"because this is what you were
meant to do with you life."

00:22:16.788 --> 00:22:18.705
And I was 13 years old.

00:22:19.952 --> 00:22:22.945
And it is kind of extraordinary
that sometimes in life,

00:22:22.945 --> 00:22:26.112
a teacher, a parent, an adult, mentor,

00:22:27.362 --> 00:22:30.192
can say just the right
words to a young person,

00:22:30.192 --> 00:22:32.206
at just the right moment,
and it can be life changing.

00:22:32.206 --> 00:22:33.520
And it was life changing for me,

00:22:33.520 --> 00:22:34.653
because I did go to New York,

00:22:34.653 --> 00:22:37.091
I did study at Julliard for two years,

00:22:37.091 --> 00:22:38.624
and then I began my career

00:22:38.624 --> 00:22:40.133
as an actor in the theater in New york.

00:22:40.133 --> 00:22:42.133
And then 12 years later,

00:22:43.321 --> 00:22:45.404
after we had met in 1974,

00:22:46.289 --> 00:22:50.608
I found out that Jack Lemmon
was coming to Broadway

00:22:50.608 --> 00:22:52.876
to do a production of Eugene O'Neill's

00:22:52.876 --> 00:22:55.017
Long Day's Journey in Tonight.

00:22:55.017 --> 00:22:58.100
And I felt at 26, I was the right age

00:23:00.311 --> 00:23:01.990
to play one of his sons.

00:23:01.990 --> 00:23:03.372
And I particularly wanted to play the role

00:23:03.372 --> 00:23:04.789
of the alcoholic,

00:23:08.028 --> 00:23:09.605
Jamie Tyrone.

00:23:09.605 --> 00:23:11.705
So I tried and tried and
tried to get an audition.

00:23:11.705 --> 00:23:12.675
I couldn't get an audition.

00:23:12.675 --> 00:23:16.207
I finally had to sort of sneak my way in.

00:23:16.207 --> 00:23:19.102
And I finally got an audition
with Jonathan Miller,

00:23:19.102 --> 00:23:20.226
who was going to direct the production,

00:23:20.226 --> 00:23:22.407
the great British director.

00:23:22.407 --> 00:23:24.535
And then I had to audition with Jack.

00:23:24.535 --> 00:23:28.341
So once again, after we
had met 12 years before,

00:23:28.341 --> 00:23:29.724
I found myself in a room with Jack Lemmon

00:23:29.724 --> 00:23:32.240
doing a scene; or we did
like four scenes together.

00:23:32.240 --> 00:23:33.721
And I was relentless with him.

00:23:33.721 --> 00:23:35.486
I'd prepared for this audition,

00:23:35.486 --> 00:23:37.713
unlike anything I'd ever prepared for.

00:23:37.713 --> 00:23:38.996
I was toppling over his lines,

00:23:38.996 --> 00:23:41.212
I was pushing him.

00:23:41.212 --> 00:23:43.440
I wanted this part so bad.

00:23:43.440 --> 00:23:45.351
And at the end of it,

00:23:45.351 --> 00:23:46.824
Jack Lemmon once again walked up to me,

00:23:46.824 --> 00:23:47.733
put his hand on my shoulder,

00:23:47.733 --> 00:23:51.181
and said, "That was a touch of terrific."

00:23:51.181 --> 00:23:52.475
He goes, "You know what,
I never thought we'd find

00:23:52.475 --> 00:23:56.873
the right kid, but Jesus
Christ what the hell was that?"

00:23:56.873 --> 00:23:58.930
And I won the role.

00:23:58.930 --> 00:24:01.094
And I got to spend the
next year of my life

00:24:01.094 --> 00:24:03.229
working on stage with my idol.

00:24:03.229 --> 00:24:06.917
And he then became the greatest mentor

00:24:06.917 --> 00:24:09.261
I could have hoped to have had.

00:24:09.261 --> 00:24:10.594
A father figure.

00:24:11.938 --> 00:24:13.628
And really somebody who,

00:24:13.628 --> 00:24:17.074
was such an extraordinary
example of a man who had reached

00:24:17.074 --> 00:24:20.813
absolute the heights in his profession,

00:24:20.813 --> 00:24:23.289
but it never went to his head.

00:24:23.289 --> 00:24:25.429
He was the sweetest man,

00:24:25.429 --> 00:24:29.405
and we then ended up doing
three films together,

00:24:29.405 --> 00:24:31.340
with Glen Gray, Glen Ross,

00:24:31.340 --> 00:24:34.682
which he used to love to
call Gene Berry, Glenn Close.

00:24:34.682 --> 00:24:36.265
It's easier to say.

00:24:37.110 --> 00:24:41.517
And you know, there's really
not a day that goes by

00:24:41.517 --> 00:24:44.028
where the influence he
had, the lessons he;

00:24:44.028 --> 00:24:46.611
just by example that he taught.

00:24:48.285 --> 00:24:50.396
I've always sort of gone through my life

00:24:50.396 --> 00:24:53.420
feeling like if I just keep
a little twist of Lemmon

00:24:53.420 --> 00:24:56.156
in my heart, then I'm going to be okay.

00:24:56.156 --> 00:24:58.575
We were talking about this
outside in the hallway.

00:24:58.575 --> 00:25:00.092
'Cause we were talking about Sean,

00:25:00.092 --> 00:25:04.265
about how sometimes you
can love what you do,

00:25:04.265 --> 00:25:05.303
and be good at it.

00:25:05.303 --> 00:25:07.805
But sometimes you don't
have to love what you do,

00:25:07.805 --> 00:25:08.721
and still be good at it.

00:25:08.721 --> 00:25:09.554
- That's Sean and acting.

00:25:09.554 --> 00:25:12.461
- Yeah, we were having
this discussion out there.

00:25:12.461 --> 00:25:16.397
And right now, not everything is great.

00:25:16.397 --> 00:25:19.306
I certainly want things; I
fight for things to be better,

00:25:19.306 --> 00:25:22.102
and I fight to do things better in myself.

00:25:22.102 --> 00:25:24.588
But I also feel like I've
been given so much opportunity

00:25:24.588 --> 00:25:28.755
that even to start to whiff of
a sort of whine or complain,

00:25:29.791 --> 00:25:33.041
as certain other people do, who I know.

00:25:34.971 --> 00:25:36.637
Little whining hosts.

00:25:36.637 --> 00:25:38.887
(laughing)

00:25:39.975 --> 00:25:41.553
I feel that it's disenchanting.

00:25:41.553 --> 00:25:43.356
- If I saw someone like
that I'd shoot 'em on sight.

00:25:43.356 --> 00:25:45.627
- Smack 'em, right.

00:25:45.627 --> 00:25:49.939
So I feel like the best thing
that I can do is be grateful,

00:25:49.939 --> 00:25:53.950
and honor it, and continue to
try to do the best work I can.

00:25:53.950 --> 00:25:57.783
And I'm fortunate that
every time someone says

00:25:58.687 --> 00:26:00.423
oh no he's not the right guy for that,

00:26:00.423 --> 00:26:03.140
and I'm able to go out and
prove that I can do it.

